# Children of the Five Winds — The Gifts of the World

**Version:** 0.4.1
**Source:** Legend of the Five Rings: Children of the Five Winds
**Publisher:** Fantasy Flight Games

---

## The Gifts of the World

Yoshimi's parched throat clenched in disappointment. The "magical oasis" was nothing more than a partly collapsed stone cistern with a small, simple pool of water. "This is…nothing more than a hole, nothing more than a latrine!" "We have served khans, shahs, poets, and holy ones," an echoing whisper said. "We've had a prince's love." Yoshimi straightened up in shock. They spied a misty figure hovering over the water, continually shifting into the shape of a serpent, then a human, then a cat or a dog. "But our water is obviously unworthy for royal and majestic lips such as yours." "Please, spirit, I spoke poorly." Yoshimi's words scrapped against their desiccated tongue. "The heat and dust poisoned my mind and manners. Forgive me." "Poets have written ghazals and songs about us." "I'm afraid I am not a fair singer…" The water sank, slowly draining away into the earth. "Wait, wait, I do know a song…" Yoshimi's shoulders sagged when the lyrics came to them. "About a foolish child who spoke without thinking…"

## Currency in the Cradle of the World

Along the Sand Road, Rokugani currency is generally accepted due to the influence of the Unicorn, but within the Qamarist Caliphate and beyond, koku, bu, and zeni become less and less common. Shopkeepers and innkeepers still accept Rokugani tender, but only for the worth of the coin's material itself, which is much less than most samurai would expect.

Instead, the most common forms of currency are the Qamari gold shenar and silver qanar, also known as a gold sun and silver moon, respectively. The colloquial names of sun and moon are holdovers from when the Empire of Rempet ruled the land. For smaller purchases, silver qanar are often cut into increments known as half- or quarter-qanars, or half- and quarter-moons. For this task, shopkeepers often carry a tool with a small curved blade at one end and a straight blade on the other, using it to cut the coins into shapes that roughly coincide with the phases of the moon.

> **THE WORTH OF ZENI**
>
> The phrase to be given zeni has come to mean being forced to deal with something unwanted, worthless, or difficult among merchant communities along the Sand Road.
> For example, a merchant coming home to the news that a demanding relative is making a surprise visit may sigh and say, "Ah, so we will be given zeni." Or an overworked scribe may one day throw their scroll and yell, "Every day I am given nothing but zeni!"
> The worth of zeni is so low that some merchants have even taken to using them to craft necklaces or other baubles that are then sold as "genuine Rokugani art" to those who travel to the Qamarist Caliphate and are curious about the mysterious Emerald Empire.

> **THE COINS OF THE EMPIRE OF REMPET**
>
> As the Qamarist Caliphate was built on the bones of the Empire of Rempet, many traces of that empire remain within the Caliphate. In particular, coins from the fallen Empire of Rempet can still be found. These coins depict the terrible visage of the Sun God and are generally considered to be cursed by those within the Caliphate. Unscrupulous merchants often try to pawn off these coins on unsuspecting travelers, hoping they will not be able to tell them apart from Qamari shenars.
> Meanwhile, brandishing this currency marks a traveler as either gullible fodder for unscrupulous merchants or as cursed by the gods of the Empire of Rempet. However, should those with a keen eye look closer, there is a secret hidden in the design of the coin.

> **THE DJINNI AND THE SCORPION QUEEN**
>
> Among items commonly found for sale along the Sand Road, there are a few copies of a tale titled The Djinni and the Scorpion Queen. Considered by most to be nothing but pure nonsense told in an interesting way, the book claims to tell the true story of a djinni who met the Queen of Scorpions, the leader of a tribe of people who are half human and half scorpion, living in sprawling hives deep under the Burning Sands.

> **BEASTS OF THE BURNING SANDS**
>
> A tome titled Beasts of the Burning Sands details in shaky handwriting a variety of preposterous and horrific creatures the author claims live within the Burning Sands. Among them are massive flesh-eating worms, human-sized scorpions with human torsos or faces, and gigantic winged serpents.

While there is no set standard of exchange between Rokugani currency and the coins of the Caliphate, the following rough equivalencies often apply: 1 Rokugani koku is considered to be worth about 1 gold shenar, and 1 bu is worth about 2 silver qanars. In addition to the shenar and qanar, the currency of the Bandar, the dira, is also commonly circulated due to the Bandar's monopoly on trade in the region. A dira is commonly treated as roughly equivalent to a half-shenar. The Sogdan, Ganzu, Nehiri, and Suhili territories also use some currencies that are holdovers from when they were independent of the Caliphate. However, while these currencies still exist, each is circulated only within its own territory and is rarely found elsewhere within the Caliphate.

## Language

Over the years, Rokugan's leaders have made significant efforts to ensure as many people speak a similar version of Rokugani as possible. Imperials understand language's power to unify a people, influencing a population's connections to identity and the past. Whether an individual or community maintains their own languages and dialects is of no large concern to the Imperials. The Great Clans have made concentrated efforts to preserve their own dialects. Many families have championed the Empire's diverse traditions, and with the realities of trade and convenience, Rokugan remains a land of many distinct languages.

The common Rokugani language is rooted in the efforts of Lady Doji, the founder of the Crane Clan. Working alongside many scholars, Doji created a unified language that she hoped would allow all the Empire's peoples to understand one another. It was rooted in many tongues of the peoples predating the Empire, and it adapted the written script of the Land of Four Rivers as well as other local scripts. The current Rokugani language has evolved over generations; while the old language has been preserved in the form of Courtly Rokugani, it has also splintered into many unique dialects and expressions for less formal use.

### OPTIONAL RULES: LANGUAGE BARRIERS

A play group that wishes to mechanically represent language barriers may adopt the guidelines presented here.

A character who is fluent in a language and wishes to communicate at length with a person who is not fluent in that language may make a TN 3 Performance check using the approach that makes the most sense for the method the player describes. Succeeding on the check means the character makes their general meaning known, although specific details may be lost in translation.

Additionally, when a character who is fluent in a language attempts any action against someone who is not fluent in that language, if being understood is an essential component, then the check's TN increases by 2. This gives a TN to actions that normally do not have one.

### Rokugani Dialects

Rokugani has many dialects throughout the Empire. Often an unknown samurai's clan can be identified simply by that samurai's lexicon. A single province may have a dozen or more dialects within it. Additionally, certain dialects are associated with wealthy nobility, while others are associated with farmers and other rural folk. Travelers usually take care not to absorb another dialect, lest they return home speaking differently and become regarded as outsiders. Because of their shared linguistic roots, Rokugani speakers can generally communicate regardless of where they are from—although varying lexicons and colloquialisms do cause misunderstandings from time to time.

### Courtly/High Rokugani

An ancient and poetic version of the Rokugani language, Courtly Rokugani uses a different alphabet, different conjugations, and more archaic vocabulary than standard Rokugani dialects. As a more complex and grandiose expression of everyday Rokugani, the courtly language is reserved primarily for ceremony, court, and most historical records. It is also prevalent in poetry and somber genres of theater. Speaking anything other than Courtly Rokugani while attending an important court is generally considered a breach of etiquette.

### Rokugani Sign Languages

Rokugani sign languages are completely distinct from spoken Rokugani, each having its own syntax and vocabulary. Of Rokugan's two most prominent sign languages, one is used primarily in the north, the other in the south. Additionally, many clans have developed their own sign languages for soundless communication, and shinobi groups use a flurry of gestures to coordinate while in the field.

### Family Ciphers

Most Great Clan families have a unique cipher—written, spoken, or signed—that is carefully guarded from generation to generation. These ciphers are designed to be unrecognizable to those outside the family and, in some cases, may predate the formation of the Empire. In this way, samurai families send communications without risk of interception. Most have stood the test of time, requiring specific instruction to understand.

## UNCOMMON LANGUAGES IN ROKUGAN

The following are uncommon or rare languages that even well-traveled Rokugani are unlikely to have heard. A Rokugani who has heard one of these languages has probably experienced it very infrequently.

### Nonhuman Languages

Rokugani often struggle with the languages spoken by nezumi and naga. Stone-Click, the language of Shadowlands nezumi, incorporates pheromones and musks alongside spoken words, aspects that are impossible for humans to replicate. The language of the Shinomen naga, River Speech, incorporates body language along with released scents that are "tasted" to convey meaning.

### Qamari Languages

The people of the massive Qamarist Caliphate speak a wide variety of languages, but the most common is Nehiri. Nehiri is divided into two specific dialects, one for day-to-day vernacular and one reserved for more formal situations. Additionally, a more poetic version of Nehiri, with refined prosody rules, has arisen with the spread of Qamarism, with the goal of further unifying followers of the Nameless Prophet.

### Ujik Languages

The many tribes of the Ujik peoples speak distinct and different languages, but these languages are similar enough with their common root that a person who is fluent in one can generally be understood by a person fluent in another. Additionally, the Ujik use a trade form of the language for commerce and legal agreements.

### Yobanjin Languages

The many Yobanjin tribes have vastly different languages from one another, existing in written and spoken forms. However, most of these have roots in the languages of the peoples who predated Rokugan, and represent natural evolutions or forks from those languages. Larger Yobanjin tribes have several dialects, and it is not uncommon for a Yobanjin tongue to have overlapping vocabulary with nearby lands, including Rokugan.

## Otherworldly Encounters

Spirits inhabit the lands of Rokugan and beyond. These mysterious beings may wander from nearby realms or inhabit specific places in the mortal world, but two things are clear: countless kinds of spirits, each with their own domains and goals, exist in the world, and each has an undeniable impact on it. Rokugani venerate many spirits, with shugenja and other priests acting as intermediaries between spiritual and human affairs. Of course, matters are not always wholly peaceable between spirits and humans in Rokugan. Some spirits, such as the animal spirits of Senkyō, can be helpful allies or tricksters who prey upon humanity, depending on circumstances, and are treated with caution or placated with offerings. Others, namely the mazoku, have tasks including the testing of human souls and the tormenting of the wicked in the afterlife; thus, while mazoku are agents of cosmic order who serve under Emma-Ō, most humans do not relish the thought of meeting one. Some spirits are even human souls who have refused to leave the Mortal Realm—remaining to complete a goal, seek revenge, or protect a loved one—or have left their living body behind to pursue some desire they cannot achieve through physical means.

Outside of Rokugan, the relationship between humans and spirits can vary in an equally interesting variety of ways. Some groups, such as the Ujik, venerate spirits like the Lords of Death, ancestors who are said to ride with their people and watch over their mortal descendants. Within the Shadowlands, corrupted spirits (kansen) may tempt even the most pious of priests with offers of power and glory beyond measure. And along the Sand Road, travelers may encounter djinn: powerful spirits with elemental affinities who are capable of great good or evil.

### RULES FOR OTHERWORLDLY ENCOUNTERS

This section covers rules for intrigues with spirits, helping GMs create evocative scenes involving supernatural beings. It includes new options for player characters to facilitate their interactions with these beings, as well as new rules for GMs to help spirit NPCs pursue their own goals in narrative and conflict scenes with the player characters. These rules build upon the rules for intrigues found on page 254 of the core rulebook.

#### Setting the Scene

If a GM knows their players are headed for an otherworldly encounter, it can help to begin to foreshadow the coming meeting with a spirit. A bit of ambient description can go a long way toward setting the mood, and describing how the environment itself grows more strange and forbidding can give an air of mystery and power to the coming scene. Consider giving a brief description like the following as the PCs approach the encounter:

- The gaps in the trees begin to grow smaller and smaller, until only thin rays of moonlight cut through the canopy.
- The temperature drops precipitously.
- The wind carries the weight of a storm upon it, yet no clouds are visible in the sky.
- The hairs on the backs of your necks stand up.
- The scent of a flower that is out of season pervades the area.
- The nearby water becomes unnaturally still, and it shows memories of the past instead of reflections.

#### Starting the Encounter

An otherworldly encounter can begin in the same manner as a normal intrigue, with the competing parties engaging one another in conversation. However, it can also be handled in a more esoteric manner: an otherworldly encounter might begin before the PCs even know they are dealing with a spirit, and determining the nature of the encounter might be the first goal they need to achieve before they can begin an intrigue.

An intrigue conflict scene can also be started if a character successfully activates the Commune with the Spirits ritual (see page 212 of the core rulebook), in which case otherworldly enounter replaces the ritual's usual effects.

### Running the Encounter

As with any other intrigue, when a GM runs an otherworldly encounter, they ask the players what they want the PCs to accomplish. These goals should be translated into social objectives, such as any of the New Mortal Social Objectives, starting this page, or unique social objectives the GM creates with the players based on these examples. Then, the GM determines what the NPCs involved want. In the case of spirits, these might be any of the New Spirit Social Objectives, or objectives the GM creates based on these examples.

Once these objectives are determined, characters take actions to pursue them, such as the Persuade action (see page 257 of the core rulebook) or any of the New Actions starting on page 130. Some actions (and some objectives) are available only to characters learned in meishōdō, the Way of Names (see Meishōdō Talismans on page 106).

### Complicating Factors

If an encounter between a group of PCs and a single spirit seems too one-sided or plain, consider tossing in other parties who can complicate the matter with their own objectives. Complicating factors might include:

### New Mortal Social Objectives

When dealing with a spirit, a mortal character can pursue additional social objectives beyond those beginning on page 254 of the core rulebook. The following are some examples of objectives mortals can pursue when dealing with spirits.

#### Create a Pact

Most spirits are good to their word—at least, more reliably so than most humans. Whether a spirit is benevolent, destructive, or even duplicitous, a pact that spirit agrees to is generally binding. If a human breaks that pact, the spirit is freed from its obligations, but rarely do spirits break their contracts.

### What Does It Take to Complete?

A character can forge a spirit pact by accumulating a number of momentum points equal to the objective's difficulty value. The difficulty value is equal to the spirit's focus value plus its intrigue or combat conflict rank plus 2 (whichever is higher).

### What Is Achieved by Completing It?

#### Dispel Possession

Possessing a mortal being is a drastic move for a spirit, but sometimes even a good-natured spirit becomes desperate enough that they feel the need to use a mortal agent to complete their goals. Many shugenja are trained in the art of breaking such possessions, forcing the spirit to either flee or take a form that can be confronted. The Possessed condition is described on page 133.

A character can dispel a spirit possessing someone else by accumulating a number of momentum points equal to the objective's difficulty value. The difficulty value is the spirit's focus plus its intrigue conflict ranks. If the spirit is possessing one of the characters pursuing this objective, the difficulty value is the spirit's focus plus its intrigue conflict ranks plus its own vigilance.

#### Invoke for Aid

While shugenja can speak to spirits directly and thus invoke their vast powers, it is possible for others to negotiate more indirectly with spirits to use their powers. Though seeking out a spirit in its place of power to beg for its assistance directly can be risky, sometimes it is the only option available to the desperate. Invoking a spirit for aid can let a character do something beyond their normal abilities, either via an existing invocation or via a display of spiritual power determined by the GM, which might exceed even what invocations can normally achieve.

A character can convince a spirit to perform an invocation of the character's choosing by accumulating a number of momentum points equal to the objective's difficulty value. The difficulty value is the spirit's intrigue conflict ranks plus the rank of the invocation the character desires it to perform (or plus 6 if the requested effect is something the GM deems to be more potent than what an invocation could normally achieve). In its place of power, a spirit can generally perform an invocation with a rank prerequisite less than the higher of its combat conflict ranks and intrigue conflict ranks. Spirits associated with one or more specific elements might only be able to perform invocations of those elements, at the GM's discretion. If a character's request is for an invocation the spirit cannot perform, the spirit should inform the character before they attempt the social objective.

At the GM's discretion, a character can also use the Invoke for Aid social objective to ask a Tainted spirit (kansen) to perform a mahō technique (see page 224 of the core rulebook). In this case, as a cost for receiving aid, the character suffers the Tainted disadvantage for the ring of the mahō technique.

#### Placate

Spirits are beings with an intellect, desires, and beliefs of their own. As a result, like people, they can become angry, dispirited, or confused. Unlike the emotions of most humans, however, the emotions of spirits shape the world around them in a tangible way. A contented spirit might help the land flourish, but even a benevolent spirit can be extremely dangerous to humans in its proximity if it becomes enraged or terrified. At the GM's discretion, the Placate objective can be pursued during a duel or skirmish to remove a spirit from the battle.

A character can calm a raging spirit by accumulating a number of momentum points equal to the objective's difficulty value. The difficulty value is the spirit's focus plus its combat conflict ranks.

#### Seal in an Object

Within Rokugan, sealing a spirit within an object is very uncommon outside of Iuchi's tradition of meishōdō. However, evil spirits are sometimes bound within objects by shugenja as a last resort, and under rare circumstances, a benevolent spirit might even consent to be bound within a physical object for a time in order to serve some greater cause. Generally, though, this objective is only pursued by practitioners of the Way of Names.

A character can seal a spirit inside of an object by accumulating a number of momentum points equal to the objective's difficulty value. The difficulty value is the spirit's focus plus double its intrigue conflict rank.

At the end of any round in which a character accumulates momentum points equal to the objective's difficulty value, the character may seal the spirit within the chosen object. The object gains the Sacred item quality, or the Unholy item quality if the spirit is Tainted, and the spirit cannot escape the object unless the object is destroyed. A character who has knowledge of meishōdō (such as by being an Iuchi Meishōdō Master or having completed the Student of Names title) and has learned the spirit's true name can instead choose to create a meishōdō talisman by completing this objective (see Meishōdō Talismans, page 106). If a character chooses not to complete this objective when they have the requisite number of momentum points, they can gain further momentum points beyond the difficulty value. Doing so can help keep a spiritual being that is sealed in a meishōdō talisman more securely confined (see Stability, page 106).

### New Spirit Social Objectives

In addition to the standard social objectives starting on page 254 of the core rulebook, spirits can pursue social objectives unique to their supernatural abilities. The following are some examples of objectives spirits can pursue.

#### Curse

Spirits sometimes play havoc with mortal lives, raining down bad luck, afflictions, or other dreadful occurrences upon them. Curses can range from minor inconveniences to deadly threats.

A spirit can curse a character by accumulating a number of momentum points equal to the objective's difficulty value. The difficulty value is equal to the character's focus plus their ranks in Theology.

> **CURSE DISADVANTAGES**
>
> A character who is cursed receives a disadvantage with the curse type. This includes any and all disadvantages with "curse" in their name. This disadvantage applies in circumstances related to the spirit's sphere of influence, and it is created using the rules for creating a custom disadvantage (see page 137 of the core rulebook). Examples of disadvantages with the curse disadvantage type can be found in Specific Disadvantages, starting on page 116 of the core rulebook. Other curses include curses of misfortune in a particular activity, such as long-distance travel or games of chance. The GM can determine if the disadvantage with the curse type is permanent or has a duration before it fades. Most curses with a duration should last for at least two weeks of gameworld time. If a curse is permanent, a PC might be able to try to make amends with the spirit to have it removed narratively, or they might complete the Invoke for Aid social objective (see page 127) on a more powerful spirit of the same affiliation or type to remove it.

#### Inspire Awe

Spirits wield powers that are difficult for humans to observe, let alone comprehend. With some effort (or sometimes unintentionally), a spirit can terrify mortals with supernatural awe, stymieing any efforts the mortals might make to sway the spirit.

A spirit can inspire awe in a character by accumulating a number of momentum points equal to the objective's difficulty value. The difficulty value is equal to the character's vigilance plus their ranks in Meditation.

### Possession

Some spirits need mortal intermediaries to perform certain actions within the Mortal Realm. They might be restricted by cosmic taboos, or they might have limitations upon their own abilities. While many spirits resolve these matters through a symbiotic relationship with humans or others who venerate them, it is not the only way for a spirit to act in the Mortal Realm. Possession is a means by which a spirit can resolve such matters more directly. Possessions are usually brief, and they do not always consist of a total bodily takeover of the host. A spirit might exert a much subtler influence on a host, guiding them toward a particular goal. Similarly, a possession might be voluntary, a host allowing a spirit to coexist inside their body for a time so the two can use their combined powers to achieve a mutual goal. See Possessed in New Conditions, page 133.

A spirit can possess a character by accumulating a number of momentum points equal to the objective's difficulty value. The difficulty value is equal to twice the sum total of the character's focus and ranks in Command.

### Request an Offering

Offerings are an important part of the relationships between mortals and spirits, because they represent the commodities humans can easily hold that supernatural beings value. As such, spirits often pursue particular offerings in their negotiations with humans.

A spirit can request an offering from a character by accumulating a number of momentum points equal to the objective's difficulty value. The difficulty value is 1 or more. A spirit can complete this objective as long as it has 1 or more momentum points.

### New Actions

The following new actions can be used by both mortal characters and spirits during otherworldly encounters. Characters (both spirit and mortal) can also use the Persuade action (page 257 of the core rulebook) in otherworldly encounters, as well as any shūji or unique actions the GM deems appropriate.

> **New Opportunities**
>
> Air 上上上 (三 symbols): You ascertain a key detail about the spirit, such as the span of its domain, its current social objective, or the terms it requests in its pacts. If you are trained in the Way of Names, you can learn its true name instead. Earth 上+: Reduce the spirit's momentum points toward its current social objective by 1. Additionally, you may reduce the spirit's momentum points toward that objective by 1 per 上上 spent this way beyond the first. Fire 上上: Treat your focus as 1 higher until the end of the scene. Water 上上: Reduce the TN of your next check targeting the spirit by 1. Void 上: Gain the Centered condition (see page 133).

### SPIRIT PACTS

Spirits and mortals can voluntarily enter into pacts, which provide benefits to both sides. The Kitsune family maintains many such pacts across the Emerald Empire, and when a pact is broken, their Naturalists and Mediators are often the ones to intercede with the spirits to restore it. After a character completes the Create a Pact social objective, the GM and players determine the specifics of three pact components: the terms, the boons, and the domain. Mortals must abide by the terms set forth in the pact; so long as they do, they receive the pact's boons while they are within the spirit's domain. In accordance with the following guidelines, the GM determines the pact's terms (on the spirit's behalf) and then the players choose the pact's boons. The domain in which the pact applies is determined by the power and influence of the spirit. The GM is the final arbiter of what constitutes an appropriate boon.

#### Set the Terms

The terms are the part of the pact that the mortals involved must heed in order to continue to receive the spirit's blessing. If the mortals violate the terms, either by failing to uphold them or by going against a taboo, the spirit may withdraw its blessing. In extreme cases, a spirit might even attempt punitive action against offending mortals who live in or near its domain, though this is unusual in Rokugan. Some example terms might include:

- Mutual Defense: A spirit might have enemies of its own from which it needs protection, and it could thus ask mortals to maintain defenses for it. Abiding by these terms does not usually mean taking up arms in its defense; instead, doing so often takes the form of maintaining barriers against invasive spiritual presences. These barriers might need to be periodically replaced, and failure to do so could allow the spirit's enemies to attack its domain.
- Periodic Offerings: A spirit might ask mortals to make periodic offerings to it based on the boon it provides. These are often symbolic, such as a cup of sake brewed from the rice the spirit helps to flourish or the first painting an artist makes every year inspired by the terrain of the spirit's domain.
- Taboos: A spirit might restrict the harvesting of resources in the region near its domain. This might mean making sure that a large stand of trees is always allowed to flourish near where the spirit makes its home, or that a certain type of animal for which it has an affinity is allowed to live without being hunted or disturbed by mortals.
- Various Tasks: Some spirits have difficulty performing tasks for themselves in the Mortal Realm, and others would simply prefer to have mortals attend to such matters. Tasks of this nature might include maintaining a shrine or set of shrines across the domain, undertaking deeds that relate to the spirit's interests in the Mortal Realm, or even trying to live up to some ideal the spirit considers important.

### Pick the Boons

Spirits often provide certain boons to mortals who inhabit their domain, with the expectation of veneration or respect for taboos in exchange. Boons might include benefits like good harvests, protection from sickness, or regular rains. When one or more PCs and a spirit make a spirit pact, choose one narrative boon and one mechanical boon, both of which take effect within the spirit's domain when the pact is finalized. GMs should feel free to create new narrative and mechanical boon options to reflect the unique abilities of spirits. These boons will be what PCs may select from when forming their spiritual pact.

#### Example Narrative Boons

The following are a few examples of narrative boons that could apply within a spirit's domain:

- Boon of Fortunate Finding: When an item is lost within the domain, it tends to turn up later at the exact moment someone needs it.
- Boon of Gentle Rains: The weather in the domain is consistent, delivering enough moisture, sun, and shade for plants and animals to flourish there.
- Boon of Good Health: Diseases are suppressed within the domain, and both their symptoms and spread (if applicable) are substantially reduced.
- Boon of Inspiration: People find the natural terrain, flora, and fauna within the domain to be especially good for inspiring works of art.
- Boon of Plenty: Harvests within the domain always generate a small surplus.
- Boon of Quiet Stars: External conflicts tend to be naturally directed away from the domain by sudden storms, flooding rivers, or other phenomena.
- Boon of Safe Harbor: Hostile Otherworldly and Tainted beings are unable to find the domain if actively searching for it, and they can enter only if invited by another being.

#### Example Mechanical Boons

The following are a few examples of mechanical boons that could apply within a spirit's domain:

- Boon of Clarity: At the start of each scene, each character gains the Centered condition (see page 133).
- Boon of Courage: At the start of each scene, each character gains the Emboldened condition (see page 133).
- Boon of Energetic Recovery: At the end of each scene, after a character removes fatigue and strife, they remove additional fatigue and strife equal to the spirit's Fire Ring.
- Boon of Permanence: When a character makes a check to resist an effect of an Otherworldly being, they can reroll a number of dice up to the spirit's Water Ring.
- Boon of Purity: When a character makes a check to resist the effect of a Tainted being, they can reroll a number of dice up to the spirit's Earth Ring. The area around the spirit's main habitation or shrine also becomes Hallowed (Spirit's Main Element) terrain.
- Boon of Restoration: When a character makes a check to heal an injury, remove a condition, or remove fatigue, they can reroll a number of dice up to the spirit's Void Ring.
- Boon of Skillful Hands: When a character makes a check to Restore, Adapt, Invent, Refine, or Attune to an item, they can reroll a number of dice up to the spirit's Air Ring.

### Define the Domain

A spirit's domain is generally the region it inhabits. The GM determines a spirit's domain based on its narrative influence and power. For most spirits, this means a spirit pact affects the nearby area. A few sample domains could include:

- A large wood around the spirit's center of power.
- A village where the primary shrine to the spirit is maintained.
- The course of a river the spirit inhabits.
- A cavern complex and mountain peak the spirit inhabits.
- The site around an item of importance to the spirit (potentially its mortal form's resting place, if it was once mortal).

## New Conditions

The following are new conditions applied by school abilities and techniques found in this book.

### CENTERED

Description: The character has cleared their mind of distractions and doubts, allowing them to focus on their task. Effects: When the character makes a check, they may remove up to 2 ⌘ from the roll results. Removed When: At the end of the scene, or after the character benefits from this condition during a check (whichever comes first).

### EMBOLDENED

Description: The character is filled with the courage to push themself beyond their normal limits and achieve things that might normally be beyond their reach. Effects: When the character makes a check, they may receive 1 fatigue to keep one additional rolled die. Removed When: At the end of the scene, or after the character benefits from this condition during a check (whichever comes first).

### POSSESSED

Description: Willingly or unwillingly, the character has come under the power of a supernatural being, which now exerts a certain influence over their actions. Effects: When the character becomes Possessed, the possessing spirit states its goal. The character can ignore that goal, but they receive 2 strife at the end of each scene in which they fully disregarded it. When the character becomes Compromised, they must unmask and then attempt to pursue the goal until the end of the scene. When the character makes a check while Possessed, they can use the spirit's skill ranks in place of their own. Additionally, if the spirit knows any invocations or other techniques, the character can use each of these techniques once per scene. Removed When: After the character spends a scene pursuing the spirit's goal, they can make a Meditation (Void) check to attempt to expel the spirit. The TN for this check is equal to the spirit's focus. If the character succeeds, the Possession ends, as the spirit is cast out. Possession can also be broken when a character achieves the Dispel Possession social objective (see Otherworldly Encounters on page 125). Alternately, the spirit can choose to end the possession at any time. After this condition is removed, the spirit might physically manifest or might flee into a Spirit Realm, at the GM's discretion.

## New Titles

The following new titles are available to characters who meet their criteria. These new titles follow the rules described on page 305 of the core rulebook.

### Animal Handler

Humans are socially flexible creatures and can bond with members of many other species, from the pet dogs, cats, and birds kept in many places to the hunting eagles trained by Ujik nomads to the war elephants trained in the Ivory Kingdoms. A character who is especially adept in handling animals might even be able to command one on the hunt or in battle, fighting side by side with their beast companion.

> **ANIMAL COMPANION ACTIONS**
>
> During combat, animal companions do not generate an initiative value or take their own actions. Instead, as a Support action, a character may instruct any number of their animal companions to each take an action. Note that the Instinctive Command title ability conferred by the Animal Handler title, once unlocked, gives a character a new option for granting actions to their animal companions.

### Doomhunter

Among the Unicorn Clan, there is one person whose role is entirely unique: the Doomhunter. A slayer of immortals, the Doomhunter is both a hero and a pariah—one who walks a path fraught with danger, regarded by their peers with apprehension and even fear.

### Imperial Peacekeeper

Although the Imperial families—the Otomo, the Miya, and the Seppun—are directly tasked with much of the mediation between clans needed to keep the Emerald Empire from fracturing into warring states, they sometimes bring outsiders into their ranks to serve as Imperial peacekeepers. This offers many advantages: members of the Great Clans are sometimes not as enmeshed in the internal politics of the Imperial Court, and they have regional expertise that may be key to negotiations. Members of Minor Clans are in some ways even better suited to the role, as they often have no stake at all in regional conflicts far from their own homes. Imperial peacekeepers serve on specific assignments for members of the Imperial families when needed, returning to their other duties when there is no crisis for them to mediate.

Assigned By: A member of the Otomo, Miya, or Seppun family with a status of 65 or higher.

Status Award: +5

Glory Award: +10

XP to Completion: 18

By Imperial Command (Title Ability): Once per scene as a Support action, you can choose a number of characters up to your ranks in Command and demand that they pull themselves together. Each chosen character removes 3 strife.

### Interpreter

Although many within Rokugan live their entire lives without thinking of the Empire's neighbors, the truth is that many other civilizations touch upon the Emerald Empire in one way or another. Some, such as the Ivory Kingdoms, have contact by way of the shared danger that is the Shadowlands. Others, such as the Seikitsu of the northern mountains, exist much nearer geographically to Rokugan but are deeply distinct in culture and way of life. And as human cultures have different languages and customs, interpreters are often required to assist in situations where these groups interact.

Interpreters are expected to become fluent not only in the languages of the people they study but also in the practices and cultural touchstones of those groups. They must be able not only to translate but also to transliterate, understanding and conveying intent beyond the literal meaning of the words. This makes becoming an excellent interpreter a challenging process, requiring not only language skills but also keen insight into human nature.

Assigned By: The GM, after a character has gained an initial familiarity with another culture and desires to learn more.

Glory Award: +5

XP to Completion: 12

Special: A character who has completed this title can be assigned it again for a new group. The new instance of the title counts as a different title, which can be completed again, conferring benefits for the new group selected.

Linguistic Liaison (Title Ability): You learn the languages (written, spoken, signed, and any other forms, as relevant) of the group for which you are an interpreter. Additionally, the first time you make a check to interact socially with a member of this group each encounter, reduce the TN by 1.

Master of the Lariat (Title Ability): When you make a Martial Arts [Melee] or Martial Arts [Ranged] Attack action check using a Snaring weapon, add 1 kept ⬠ set to ◒ ⚔.

### Student of Names

When Iuchi set out from Rokugan, he discovered that spirits of other lands had different values, desires, and observable traits than the spirits of his homeland of Rokugan. However, despite these spirits' wide variety of natures and properties—they could be anything from ancestral spirits to cosmic guardians to diabolical tricksters—all of them seemed to have one thing in common: a true name. With this name, Iuchi could not merely request a spirit's attention but demand it. He could not only make contracts with a spirit but wield its power as his own or bind the spirit within a talisman.

For a people constantly on the move, like Iuchi's, this method made it possible to bring spiritual power with them wherever they went on their long journeys. A master of meishōdō can invoke spirits in more traditional ways, via offerings and contracts, when they desire to, relying on their training in the Way of Names only when dealing with especially dangerous beings. Most hold the use of true names in reserve, for few spirits like to be commanded, and sealed or not, an angry spirit poses a threat that can last generations.

While meishōdō was not the only sorcerous art Iuchi mastered, it was surely the greatest achievement that he passed down to the Unicorn Clan. While only a few in Rokugan have had a chance to learn Iuchi's method, it can be taught to those willing to learn it. Assigned By: A shugenja trained in Iuchi's method who has a status of 55 or higher, such as the daimyō of the Iuchi family, one of its promising scions, or the ancient and mysterious Grandfather Iuchi.

### Scarlet Banners Ranger

Tasked with guarding the great city of Khanbulak, the Scarlet Banners are garrisoned in the city and serve as enforcers of the law there and across large swaths of the surrounding region. While many of the Scarlet Banners' leaders see the posting as a waste of the talents of some of the Unicorn's most skilled fast cavalry, the job is not without its rigors. Members of the Scarlet Banners must be able to ride into small towns across Unicorn lands, mediate disputes between commoners, understand and enforce the law, and do so without any expectation of immediate backup from Khanbulak. Most members of the Scarlet Banners are more than capable of operating in a unit of two to three, or with only their steed as a partner. Assigned By: A leader within the White Horde of status 55 or higher. Status Award: +5 Glory Award: +10 XP to Completion: 24

When another character makes a check to resist an effect of an Attack action you perform (including a check to resist a critical strike you inflict as part of the attack), increase the TN of the check to resist by 1.

### White Guard Veteran

The White Guard comprise the most elite cavalry unit within the White Horde of the Unicorn Clan. They are known for the power of their heavily armored riders and steeds and their willingness to face long odds with grim determination. Members of the White Guard are trained to stand firm even against supernatural foes, for one of their duties is hunting the Moto who were lost to the Shadowlands alongside the dreaded Moto Tsume.

## Tenet of Bushido: Compassion

A samurai's station gives them significant social and martial power over the common population. It would be easy to exploit this position for personal gain, and indeed, many do. However, the Bushidō tenet of Compassion reminds the warrior of their humble purpose and their responsibility to humankind. Compassion is concern for the well-being of others—the desire to alleviate another's suffering. It is not merely having sympathy for another's misfortune but altruistically acting out of that concern. Without Compassion, the warrior is merely another base killer in a world that is already filled with tragedy and injustice. A samurai uses their strength to preserve human dignity, showing mercy and respect to defeated opponents and acting for the betterment of society. As one samurai's actions can affect tens of thousands of people, it is important that a samurai always be mindful of their actions.

Yet Compassion in one situation can be cruelty under different circumstances. Perhaps more than other tenets, Compassion is defined as much by intention as it is by an action's outcome. What is right and wrong may be clear in plays and songs and courtly debate, but as all warriors soon discover, in practice, it is never so simple.

### Defining Compassion

Bushidō means "warrior's path," and all of its tenets are seen through the eyes of warriors. In this context, the tenet of Compassion refers specifically to a warrior's compassion. This means a samurai must comply with the ethics of preserving human dignity balanced with the realities of the samurai way of life. For instance, killing an opponent in a single strike may appear brutal, but it is widely considered to be the most merciful end to a fight, as it spares one's foe the unnecessary suffering of a much slower death. Returning a defeated foe's blades to their family—a longstanding samurai practice—is also considered a compassionate act, as it acknowledges the loss one's actions have inflicted and shows respect for the family of the vanquished. In some cases, appearing stoic before the distressed is not callousness but an act of Compassion—when it allows them to maintain their dignity.

Compassionate acts are defined by their helpfulness and benevolence—a compassionate warrior feels empathy for ally and adversary alike. Compassionate acts are inherently merciful and altruistic, demonstrating kindness without care for how they make the samurai appear. In this way, Compassion is tied to the Bushidō virtues of Courtesy and Sincerity. The warrior does what they can to lessen the suffering caused by a person's circumstances, purely out of concern for others.

### Muddied Waters

What qualifies as compassionate is a point of fierce debate, often delving into fringe cases of ethical quandaries. For many samurai, all that Compassion demands is empathy and benevolence—to protect the peasantry, abstain from unjust cruelty, and show grace in victory. For others, this alone is not substantial enough to satisfy a warrior's adherence to Compassion. Compassion must be demonstrated by going out of one's way to help others and by finding a benevolent and merciful solution at every opportunity, no matter the circumstance. After all, what good is a sword that is never drawn—or for that matter, remains sheathed when it could defend against suffering?

But is it compassionate to spare an opponent's life when death is preferable to the consequences of defeat? Does it show a disregard for Compassion to tell a truth that would ruin a good person's reputation or to mete out a just punishment because it is harsh? And what use is Compassion if the one whose life is spared goes on to threaten or destroy the things the samurai has sworn to protect?

The teachings of the Unicorn Clan offer a simple answer to this complex question: A warrior's Compassion should either "stay the hand or sting the heart." What this means is that the warrior should spare pain and suffering whenever possible, and when not possible, should share in another's pain—to demonstrate remorse, to care for those who have been displaced, to help repair what they have destroyed, and if necessary, to bear the burden of being hated. These teachings suggest that there are no true enemies: just individuals who, due to circumstance, now oppose you. While not all samurai agree with this position, it has influenced the eternal debate, and most agree that without people to protect, the warrior has no purpose—and therefore, no true power.

## TRIFLING BREACHES AND SACRIFICES

The Legend of the Five Rings roleplaying game core rulebook contains guidelines for gaining honor for virtuous deeds as well as for forfeiting honor to act disgracefully (see page 300 of the core rulebook). However, less portentous acts can affect a character's honor as well. A samurai ideally adheres to Bushidō in all aspects of their life, and many small demonstrations eventually add up. The following optional additions supplement Table 7–1: Honor and Glory Forfeits/Awards, on page 300 of the core rulebook, and the examples related to Compassion, on page 301 of the core rulebook.

### Trifling Breaches

When a character wishes to commit a breach of Compassion that is judged to be less serious than a minor breach, they must first forfeit 1 honor. Examples of trifling breaches of Compassion:

- Purposely undermining another's dignity through prank or inaction.
- Being unmoved by the burdens of someone less privileged in station.
- Neglecting to perform a simple act that would lessen another's distress, danger, or difficulty.
- Ridiculing another for prioritizing compassionate acts.

### Trifling Sacrifices

When a character makes a sacrifice in the name of Compassion, but it is judged to be less significant than a minor sacrifice, they are awarded 1 honor. Examples of trifling sacrifices of Compassion:

## New NPC Templates

The following new NPC templates allow GMs to turn generic NPCs into ones whom the PCs might be likely to encounter in the lands of the Unicorn or Fox. Each template works the same way as the templates on page 311 of the core rulebook.

### FOX CLAN SAMURAI

### TRICKSTER ANIMAL IN DISGUISE

## New Demeanor: Compassionate

The following demeanor is designed to reflect a personality type common among the willful peoples of the Unicorn Clan, along with other samurai who strongly embrace the Bushidō tenet of Jin: Compassion for those of less privileged station, and even for one's enemies. These samurai avoid inflicting needless suffering, believe in social obligation, demonstrate mercy whenever possible, and strive to use their power for the betterment of everyone. The Compassionate demeanor works in the same manner as the other demeanors on page 310 of the core rulebook.

Social Skill Check TN Modifiers: Fire +2, Air –1, Water –1

Common Way of Unmasking: Hasty Action

This person acts immediately on their outrage and frustration, usually without thinking things through. Disregarding any consequences, they attempt the shortest path to what they want, even if they will regret it later.

### UNICORN CLAN SAMURAI

## Horse Templates

As the dedicated masters of steeds throughout the Empire, the Unicorn families maintain their own unique horse breeds both for trade and their own use. GMs can represent horses from these breeds by applying the following templates to the Horse (Rokugani Pony) NPC profile on page 326 of the core rulebook. Additionally, the Wild Desert Horse and Ujik War Pony can be found in this section.

Rules for standard mounts are contained in the Unicorn Warhorses sidebar, on page 326 of the core rulebook.

### IDE TRAVELING PONY

The Ide maintain the second-largest horse and pony herds in the Unicorn Clan, but their horses are ill-suited to war. While smaller and more mild-tempered than other Unicorn steeds, Ide traveling ponies are extremely hardy; they can subsist on little forage, endure a wide variety of climates, and travel long distances with modest burdens on their backs. The Ide widely trade surplus traveling ponies as goods. As these ponies are prized traveling companions, there is high demand for the breed throughout the Empire.

An Ide from the Ide Trader, Ide Emissary, or Ide Messenger schools may replace their horse or Unicorn warhorse with an Ide traveling pony. To represent an Ide traveling pony, apply this template to the Horse (Rokugani Pony) profile:

### IUCHI RIDING STEED

The smaller steeds bred by the Iuchi family are bred not for speed or power, but for intelligence. Iuchi riding steeds are so intelligent that they can be trained to respond to a variety of commands, both verbal and from hand gestures in sign language. Stories of horses assisting with complex math problems are probably apocryphal.

An Iuchi Meishōdō Master may replace their Unicorn warhorse with an Iuchi riding steed. To represent an Iuchi riding steed, apply this template to the Horse (Rokugani Pony) profile:

> **HORSE AND PONY NPCS**
>
> The following steed options appear in the Legend of the Five Rings roleplaying game core rulebook:
> - Horse (Rokugani Pony): Represents the typical horse bred and ridden throughout Rokugan (page 326).
> - Unicorn Warhorse: See Horse [Rokugani Pony] profile and Unicorn Warhorses sidebar, pages 326 and 327.
> - Utaku Steed: One of the wild blessed steeds of the Utaku family, specifically from the Blessed Herd (page 327).

Additionally, the following steed option appears in Path of Waves: The Essential Guide to Rōnin:

> **PLAYER CHARACTER STEEDS**
>
> Characters who are mounted on steeds have a few advantages during scenes. These are listed on page 326 of the core rulebook and are repeated here. During a conflict, a character's mount does not take a separate turn, but instead provides the following benefits to its rider:
> - When the rider succeeds on a Movement action check, add bonus successes equal to the mount's Water Ring.
> - The rider may use their Survival skill in place of their Fitness skill for any Fitness check they make.

New Rule: While the character is dismounted, their mount may take one independent action during the character's turn. During narrative and downtime scenes, a mounted character doubles their overland travel speed.

### MOTO CHARGER

The Moto claim the largest horses of any Unicorn family—larger even than the famed Utaku steeds—and they freely trade breeding stock with the peoples outside Rokugan's borders. As a consequence, the Moto have a diverse array of hoofstock suitable for diverse needs. The most famous of these is the Moto charger, a hot-tempered breed known for its strength, size, and explosive gallop, which comes at the cost of stamina. It can carry a heavily armored Moto lancer into the thickest battle, but for prolonged journeys, most warriors ride something else. A Moto Conqueror may replace their Unicorn warhorse with a Moto charger. To represent a Moto charger, apply this template to the Horse (Rokugani Pony) profile:

#### SHINJO COURSER

Shinjo coursers are most closely related to the breeds common in Qamari territory. These breeds are renowned for their speed and stamina, but they are also high-spirited and flighty. They are ideal steeds for scouts, courtiers, and mounted archers, but ill-suited to traditional battle. A Shinjo Outrider or Shinjo Mounted Archer may replace their Unicorn warhorse with a Shinjo courser. To represent a Shinjo courser, apply this template to the Horse (Rokugani Pony) profile: Conflict Rank: Combat +1 Endurance: +2 Advantages (add): Fleet of Foot (Water) [Martial; Physical) Demeanor: Cautious [Air +2, Earth –2]

#### UTAKU WARHORSE

Famous for their superior steeds, the Utaku family are widely regarded as the foremost horse breeders and trainers throughout the Great Clans. But this is not merely because of their access to steeds from the famous Blessed Herd—indeed, Utaku steeds are wild horses and are not trained or bred in the traditional manner—but because of their excellent warhorse breeds, an ideal blend of power, speed, and stamina. These warhorses set the standard for the "Unicorn warhorse" and are a symbol of prestige within the clan, widely traded to Unicorn samurai of all descriptions. The default Unicorn warhorse is, in fact, an Utaku warhorse. PCs from any Unicorn family may have one as part of their starting package. To represent a Unicorn warhorse, apply this template to the Horse (Rokugani Pony) profile on page 326 of the core rulebook: Conflict Rank: Combat +1 Endurance: +4 Composure: +4 Advantages (add 1): Fearsome Presence (Fire) [Social; Interpersonal], Masterful Fighter (Earth) [Martial; Mental], Veteran's Instincts (Water) [Scholar; Mental] Disadvantages (remove): Skittish

### New NPCs

The profiles in this section offer additional options for GMs to use as NPCs in their Legend of the Five Rings games.

#### ANIMALS

In the following section are several animal and beast profiles, some of which may be used for various school abilities, passions, or encounters.

### TRAMPLE

The aurochs is a silhouette 4 creature. When an aurochs inflicts a critical strike, the TN of the check to resist is 6, and if the target fails their check to resist, the target suffers the Dazed condition in addition to the other effects.

### Badger

Solitary and burrowing creatures, badgers can be found throughout Rokugan. Their pelts and meat are prized in some communities, while in others it is considered bad luck to kill one. Badgers tend to be shy, but when threatened, they are known to ferociously defend themselves and their young. As with foxes, it is believed that many badgers are actually shapeshifting tricksters, although legends say mujina are less likely to seek out human beings except when they are testing their skill with illusion.

> **Favored Weapons & Gear (Badger)**
>
> Claws: Range 0–1, Damage 4, Deadliness 3, Razor-Edged

### Abilities (Badger)

### Giant Bat

While the giant bats of Rokugan are intimidating to behold, flying at night with wingspans nearing five feet and foxlike features (hence their colloquial name of "flying foxes"), in truth they are docile creatures that feed on fruits and nectar. Bats are considered good luck in Rokugani culture, especially since the Rokugan word for bat, komori, can be spelled in a way that reads "abundance of happiness." However, orchard farmers rarely regard them with unmitigated pleasure, as they have been known to devastate certain fruit harvests.

> **Favored Weapons & Gear (Giant Bat)**
>
> Bite: Range 0, Damage 2, Deadliness 1, Razor-Edged

### Abilities (Giant Bat)



### Table 3-1: Trade Goods along the Sand Road

| FOOD AND DRINK |  |  |  | (section header) |
| Street food | 1 | 4 zeni or 3 quarter-qanars | 6 zeni or 5 quarter-qanars | In every city are stalls selling cheap food, and along the Burning Sands, it is no different. From fried chickpea patties served wrapped in flatbread to shish kebabs of spiced vegetables to a local delicacy of grilled sheep's-milk cheese, there is no end to the delectable delicacies. |
| Food served at inns or restaurants | 1 | 5 zeni or 1 qanar | 8 zeni or one and a half qanars | In inns and restaurants throughout the Caliphate, it is common to see fava bean and chickpea soup; flatbreads topped with fresh, aromatic herbs; and grape leaves stuffed with rice and vegetables. |
| Dessert | 2 | 3 zeni or 2 quarter-qanars | 6 zeni or 5 quarter-qanars | At street stalls and inns, you can find buttery pastry topped with honey and nuts; candied cherries and pomegranate seeds; and a cake-like dish made from sweet sheep's-milk cheese and rose- or orange-blossom water. |
| Suutai tsai tea | 3 | 2 zeni or 1 quarter-qanar | 13 zeni or 5 half-qanars | Often served in Ujik settlements, this tea is made from milk, tea leaves, and salt. This drink is also used as a base for soups with the addition of vegetables and dumplings. |
| Arkhi | 1 (among the Ujik); otherwise 6 | 4 zeni or 3 quarter-qanars | 6 zeni or 5 quarter-qanars | While the Caliphate bans alcohol, the Ujik have no such qualms about producing and consuming liquor. This drink is made from alcohol and fermented mare's milk. It is commonly found among the Ujik and at inns that serve them. |
| Arak | 6 | 2 zeni or 1 quarter-qanar | 13 zeni or 5 half-qanars | While alcohol is forbidden by the laws of the Caliphate, the hidden sale of liquor remains, often under the guise of a medicine or elixir. This liquor is one such item. Made from grapes and anise seed, it has a strong licorice or herbal taste. Its milky-white appearance and strong potency have given it the nickname lion's milk. |
| Sea-buckthorn juice | 2 | 2 zeni or 1 quarter-qanar | 13 zeni or 5 half-qanars | The sea buckthorn is a hearty plant that bears orange fruit around the size of a thumb. It is popular as one of the only fruits that grow naturally in the rockier parts of the Burning Sands. |
| CLOTHING |  |  |  | (section header) |
| Nehiri robes | 2 | 1 koku or 1 gold shenar | 3 koku or 3 gold shenars | These loose-fitting robes are perfect for comfortable wear in the desert heat and come in bright, eye-catching colors. They are often worn with thick, beaded belts and special pointed shoes known as balga. |
| Bandar kaftan and leather overlay | 6 | 5 koku or 5 gold shenars | 10 koku or 10 gold shenars | These loose, neutral-colored kaftans are worn with sturdy, camel-leather overlays. The overlays are often tiled like a samurai's armor or cut into scalelike pieces. This style of clothing was developed for wear in Bandar trade caravans, allowing the wearer to blend in with the sands while the leather overlay adds protection and offers a convenient place to discreetly hide weapons. |
| Sogdan coat | 6 | 10 koku or 10 gold shenars | 30 koku or 30 gold shenars | These elegantly patterned coats are particularly eye-catching, incorporating bright colors and intricate embroideries. It is common to see Sogdan coats in places of high learning such as libraries, courts, and academic institutions, where the more elaborate the coat, the higher educated the wearer is assumed to be. |
